Tell people when a newer release exists, once a day, and let them turn it off.

Aerium has no update infrastructure. Chrome has Omaha on Windows and a distro
package on Linux; ungoogled-chromium has neither, and an AppImage has nothing at
all. So someone who installed once has no way to learn that a security fix
shipped except by opening the releases page and remembering their own version
number, which in practice means running an old browser indefinitely. That is a
worse outcome, including for privacy, than one connection a day.

The checker is the same header the Android repository already carries, byte for
byte, and it is registered the same way: beside the browsing-data lifetime
manager in chrome_browser_main_extra_parts_profiles.cc, which is the closest
thing already there - another profile-scoped service that exists to run on a
timer rather than to answer a call. ServiceIsCreatedWithBrowserContext() is what
makes that work, because nothing ever asks for this service and a lazily built
one would never be built.

What it deliberately does not do is download or install anything. Replacing a
running binary is a different feature with a different risk profile - signature
verification, an AppImage that has to rewrite itself, an elevated installer on
Windows - and shipping half of it would be worse than shipping none. This tells
you, and points at the release; the install is yours.

Each platform asks about its own repository, chosen by BUILDFLAG inside the
header, because a Linux AppImage and a Windows build of the same Chromium are
different artifacts on different cadences. Which is also why the desktop file is
identical to the Android one rather than adapted: one file for three platforms
makes any divergence a diff rather than a thing to remember.

Two rows on the About page, next to the version they are about - which on this
build means directly under it, ungoogled having already removed the "Get help
using Chrome" row that used to follow. The switch states the privacy cost before it states the purpose, because
"contacts GitHub once a day" is the part someone turning it off cares about. The
result row exists only when there is a result - the checker writes an empty
version string to mean "this build is the newest one", so a permanent "you are
up to date" never appears and a result that has been superseded disappears by
itself.

Three details that are easy to get wrong here.

The switch binds through pref-key rather than through a prefs object. About is a
Lit page composing PrefServiceObserverMixinLit, not PrefsMixin, and it is handed
no prefs - but settings-toggle-button composes PrefKeyObserverMixin, which
mirrors the named pref itself, so the binding is self-sufficient. The result row
cannot use that: it reads two prefs to decide whether it exists at all, so it
mirrors them the same way connectedCallback already mirrors feedback_allowed.

All three prefs go into the settingsPrivate allowlist. A pref missing from
prefs_util.cc is not an error the UI can see - the write silently does nothing
and the switch looks like it saves. They are spelled as literals rather than
taken from the header, because including it there would drag the network service
and the JSON decoder into a target with no business depending on either; the
comment says they must match, which is what the Android side does too.

OpenWindowProxyImpl leaves the _google_chrome block it was in. The result row
opens a URL on every build, not only on branded ones, and a second unconditional
import of the same module is a duplicate identifier rather than a fallback.

And the deps the header needs are declared on the target that compiles it,
//chrome/browser/profiles:profiles_extra_parts_impl. Only
//services/network/public/cpp was already there. The rest would have built
anyway - everything named is linked into chrome regardless - which is exactly
why they are worth writing down: a missing dep is invisible until it is
expensive, and net/traffic_annotation is not reachable through //net and has
cost this project a build already. That target now goes through gn check, and
the pre-flight compiles the object, so both halves of the claim are checked
rather than asserted.

One hunk here is not about the updater. ungoogled's disable-ai patch removes
tree_fixing::AXTreeFixingServicesRouterFactory's call site from this file and
its dep from the target, and leaves the #include behind. Nothing uses it, and
gn check - which this change switches on for this target - refuses it, so the
dead include comes out. It has to be in this patch rather than beside it: the
check and the thing the check trips over arrive together.

